From 21da9fc0fb2a355ba26d74198d1e4e64312cc731 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:11:31 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 15/90] perf(visualizer): cut the mesh shader's pixel budget on
 Android, name the layers in the HUD

The GPU is the binding constraint, and it is measurable from the outside: the same
build pins the visualizer at 30 fps on the phone this app targets and stutters far
less on a flagship SoC. So the work is to spend fewer fragments and fewer
full-screen passes per frame, not to look for more JS.

This round:

- The latent background renders TWO full-screen WebGL shaders, and they are not
  equal citizens. The mesh gradient is a smooth colour field with one
  low-frequency noise term, so a coarse backing store upscales invisibly; the
  dithering layer draws its 4x4 pattern in device pixels
  (`u_pxSize * u_pixelRatio` against `gl_FragCoord`) and aliases the moment it
  loses resolution. They now have separate budgets: the mesh drops to 640x360 on
  Android (230,400 px, -75 % against the old 921,600) while the dithering layer
  keeps 1280x720 everywhere.

- The frame HUD now names what it measured: `vis`, `bg` and `glow`. A frame-time
  reading is only actionable next to the layers that produced it - "30 fps in the
  visualizer" is a different bug with the shader background than without it, and
  there is no adb on the target phone to look anything up on. This also makes the
  previous commit's glow change verifiable from a screenshot (`glow: compositor`
  vs `shadow`).

utils/platform.ts gains `isAndroidRuntime` (packaged shell OR Android user agent)
so quality-for-frame-time trade-offs ask about the device rather than about how
the page was delivered; the glow switch now uses it too, and it is pinned apart
from `isAndroidShell` by test/unit/utils/platform.test.ts.
